1.3.m:
What of the gāyatra is above the hiṅkāra, that is immortal.
Therein he should place himself, and also the sacrificer;
and as for any other sāman, [he should place them] above its pratihāra.

1.4.m:
Now some make this hiṅkāra outside, as it were: 'him bhā ovā'.
Prosperity indeed is outside, as it were;
prosperity indeed is the hiṅkāra of the sāman.

1.14.m:
That is the sāman that hears the gods.
All deities indeed listen to one who knows thus for what is auspicious and good;
they cause him to do only what is auspicious and good.

Sucitta Śailana used to say: ‘Let him who desires sacrifice choose only me;
thereupon sacrifice will attend him.
For when one who knows thus chants the udgītha, all deities are satisfied in turn;
they, being satisfied, will do so for him that sacrifice will attend him.’
Fourth khaṇḍa in the third anuvāka.
The third anuvāka is completed.

1.16.m:
They said, ‘Let us sing the sāman upon the ṛk.’
Running back again, they sang the sāman upon the ṛk;
thereby they drove away the asuras from this world.

1.23.m:
Prajāpati squeezed this speech.
From it, when squeezed, essence flowed forth;
those became these worlds.

He squeezed these worlds.
From them, when squeezed, essence flowed forth;
those became these deities: Agni, Vāyu, that Āditya.

He squeezed these deities.
From them, when squeezed, essence flowed forth;
that became the threefold knowledge.

He squeezed the threefold knowledge.
From it, when squeezed, essence flowed forth;
those became these mystic utterances: bhūḥ, bhuvaḥ, svaḥ.

He squeezed these mystic utterances.
From them, when squeezed, essence flowed forth;
that became this syllable: om, which is this.

1.24.m:
And because, being a syllable (akṣara), it did not decay (nākṣīyata), therefore it is imperishable (akṣaya).
'Imperishable' (akṣaya) indeed is its name;
they call it 'syllable' (akṣara) cryptically.

Now some sing this as 'om'.
One should not sing it like that;
for [the chant] is liable to become separated from this essence;
moreover, in that case, there would be as it were two [syllables]: 'o-m'.
And some sing it as 'o'.
That, however, is not sung [properly];
one should not sing it in that way either.
One should sing simply 'oṃ';
thus one unites it with this essence.

Thereby one satisfies this essence.
The essence, satisfied, satisfies the syllable;
the syllable, satisfied, satisfies the mystic utterances;
the mystic utterances, satisfied, satisfy the Vedas;
the Vedas, satisfied, satisfy the deities;
the deities, satisfied, satisfy the worlds;
the worlds, satisfied, satisfy the syllable;
the syllable, satisfied, satisfies speech;
speech, satisfied, satisfies space;
space, satisfied, satisfies creatures.
He is satisfied with offspring and cattle who knows this thus, and also [the sacrificer] for whom one knowing thus sings the udgītha.
Second khaṇḍa in the seventh anuvāka.
The seventh anuvāka is completed.

1.25.m:
He rises on the farther shore of the ocean.
Rising indeed, he mounts upon the back of the wind;
he rises from the immortal itself;
he moves along the immortal;
he is established in the immortal.

Now, that which is white is the form of speech, of the ṛgverse, of Agni, of death.
That which is speech is the ṛgverse;
and that which is Agni is death.

Then that which is black is the form of the waters, of food, of the mind, of the yajus-formula.
Those waters are food;
and that mind is the yajus-formula.

1.26.m:
Now, that which is white is the form of speech, of the ṛgverse, of Agni, of death.
That which is speech is the ṛgverse;
and that which is Agni is death.

Then that which is black is the form of the waters, of food, of the mind, of the yajus-formula.
Those waters are food;
and that mind is the yajus-formula.

Then the person who is [there] is breath, that sāman, that brahman, that immortal.
That breath is the sāman;
and that brahman is the immortal.

1.27.m:
Now, this is the person who is here in the eye;
he who is in Āditya is the over-person;
he who is in the lightning is the supreme person.

He who is here in the eye is named the conforming one (anurūpa);
for he conforms to all forms.
One should venerate him as the conforming one;
all forms conform to him.

He who is in Āditya is the counterpart (pratirūpa);
for he faces all forms.
One should venerate him as the counterpart;
all forms turn toward him.

1.33.m:
One acts with speech, leads with breath, causes to go with mind.
This mind is that which is restrained;
by it, wherever he desires, there he places himself and the sacrificer.

Four are the one, four are the other;
they are eight.
The gāyatrī has eight syllables;
the sāman is gāyatra, and the gāyatrī is brahman;
thus it attains to brahman.
Cattle have eight hoofs; thereby it is conducive to cattle.
First khaṇḍa in the eleventh anuvāka.

1.34.m:
This is the course of Āditya, this of the Moon.
Four are these, four are those;
they are eight.
The gāyatrī has eight syllables;
the sāman is gāyatra, and the gāyatrī is brahman;
thus it attains to brahman.
Cattle have eight hoofs; thereby it is conducive to cattle.

1.35.m:
These two ends join together;
in accordance with this, the year is endless.
Its two ends are winter and spring.
In accordance with this, the two ends of a village meet together;
in accordance with this, the two ends of a necklace meet together;
in accordance with this, a serpent lies having brought its coils together around.

1.36.m:
Now this sāman in Parjanya:
The east wind is its hiṅkāra;
that it causes clouds to surge together is the prastāva;
that it thunders is the udgītha;
that it lightens is the pratihāra;
that it rains is the nidhana.

1.45.m:
Then Pṛthu Vainya asked the divine Vrātyas:
'They call Indra the uktha, the ṛc, the udgītha, brahman, the sāman, breath, out-breath;
or mind, sight, down-breath do they call [him], hearing—the learned theologians speak of him in many ways.'

1.56.m:
Regarding this there is this verse:
"A woman indeed in the beginning wandered in the flood seeking a husband;
for seventy thousand years; then was born the Seer."

2.2.m:
Now then begins the procreation of ṛc and sāman:
When he utters the hiṅkāra, he roars toward [her] by that;
when he chants the prastāva, he leaps onto [her] by that;
when he takes up the ādi, he emits semen by that;
when he chants the udgītha, he causes the emitted semen to develop by that;
when he chants the pratihāra, he makes the developed semen grow by that;
when he chants the upadrava, he gives form to the grown semen by that;
when he reaches the nidhana, he brings forth the formed semen by that;
this is the procreation of ṛc and sāman.

2.3.m:
They desired to reach it through speech;
they mounted speech;
he took hold around their speech;
therefore speech is taken hold of around;
for with it one speaks both truth and untruth.

They desired to reach it through mind;
they mounted mind;
he took hold around their mind;
therefore mind is taken hold of around;
for with it one thinks both good and evil.

They desired to reach it through sight;
they mounted sight;
he took hold around their sight;
therefore sight is taken hold of around;
for with it one sees both what is worthy to be seen and what is unworthy to be seen.

They desired to reach it through hearing;
they mounted hearing;
he took hold around their hearing;
therefore hearing is taken hold of around;
for with it one hears both what is worthy to be heard and what is unworthy to be heard.

They desired to reach it through downward breath;
they mounted downward breath;
he took hold around their downward breath;
therefore downward breath is taken hold of around;
for with it one smells both fragrant and foul odors.

2.4.m:
'Origin' (ābhūti), say the Kārīrādayas;
for following breath, offspring and cattle come into being;
he who thus venerates this as 'origin' becomes [plentiful] with breath, offspring, and cattle.

'Union' (sambhūti), says Sātyayajñi;
for following breath, offspring and cattle unite;
he who thus venerates this as 'union' thrives with breath, offspring, and cattle.

'Power' (prabhūti), say the Śailanas;
for following breath, offspring and cattle come forth;
he who thus venerates this as 'power' comes forth indeed with breath, offspring, and cattle.

'Being' (bhūti), say the Bhāllabins;
for following breath, offspring and cattle are;
he who thus venerates this as 'being' is indeed [rich] in breath, offspring, and cattle.

'The excluded one, unexcluded,' says Pārṣṇa Śailana;
for this excludes the other, another does not exclude this;
he excludes his hating rival who knows thus.
Second khaṇḍa in the second anuvāka.

2.5.m:
'The sole hero,' says Āruṇeya;
for breath is the only hero;
one heroic, vigorous son is born to him who knows thus.

2.8.m:
The asuras said: “Come, let us find out who this is that has treated us thus.”
They came thither;
having approached, they saw him.
